database: write-ahead log, synced at checkpoints

On an idle printer HelixScreen writes one small row (helix-screen
local_screen seen_at) every 10 minutes. With SQLite's defaults each
commit created a rollback journal, synced it twice, synced the directory,
wrote and synced the database and deleted the journal: on ext4 each sync
is a journal commit, about 100 KB on the eMMC for a few bytes of data,
14 MB or more a day. Now journal_mode=WAL (kept in the database file)
and synchronous=NORMAL: a commit appends to moonraker-sql.db-wal and is
synced at checkpoints (every 1000 pages) or when Moonraker closes. A
power cut can lose the last commits, not the database. Backup and
restore use SQLite's backup API, which works with WAL.

Upstream: not submitted (AD5X open OS)

--- a/moonraker/components/database.py	2026-11-09 10:04:40.060700938 -0500
+++ b/moonraker/components/database.py	2026-11-09 10:04:40.070198349 -0500
@@ -754,6 +754,13 @@
         self.sync_conn = sqlite3.connect(
             str(db_path), timeout=1., detect_types=sqlite3.PARSE_DECLTYPES
         )
+        # Kiln OS: write-ahead log, synced at checkpoints only. A small
+        # commit (HelixScreen's presence row every 10 min) was a rollback
+        # journal created, synced twice and deleted, each sync an ext4
+        # journal commit on the eMMC. journal_mode is kept in the file;
+        # synchronous is per connection.
+        self.sync_conn.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL")
+        self.sync_conn.execute("PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL")
         self.sync_conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row
         self.setup_database()
 
@@ -777,6 +784,7 @@
         conn = sqlite3.connect(
             str(self._db_path), timeout=1., detect_types=sqlite3.PARSE_DECLTYPES
         )
+        conn.execute("PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL")  # Kiln OS: see above
         conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row
         while True:
             future, func, args = self.command_queue.get()
